#=GF RL   Protein Sci. 2009;18:694-706.
#=GF RN   [9]
#=GF RM   23918047
#=GF RT   Binding of a pleurotolysin ortholog from Pleurotus eryngii to
#=GF RT   sphingomyelin and cholesterol-rich membrane domains.
#=GF RA   Bhat HB, Kishimoto T, Abe M, Makino A, Inaba T, Murate M, Dohmae
#=GF RA   N, Kurahashi A, Nishibori K, Fujimori F, Greimel P, Ishitsuka R,
#=GF RA   Kobayashi T;
#=GF RL   J Lipid Res. 2013;54:2933-2943.
#=GF RN   [10]
#=GF RM   15245918
#=GF RT   Cloning, expression, and pore-forming properties of mature and
#=GF RT   precursor forms of pleurotolysin, a sphingomyelin-specific
#=GF RT   two-component cytolysin from the edible mushroom Pleurotus
#=GF RT   ostreatus.
#=GF RA   Sakurai N, Kaneko J, Kamio Y, Tomita T;
#=GF RL   Biochim Biophys Acta. 2004;1679:65-73.
#=GF DR   INTERPRO; IPR009413;
#=GF DR   TC; 1.C.97;
#=GF DR   SO; 0100021; polypeptide_conserved_region;
#=GF CC   This family consists of several bacterial and fungal
#=GF CC   Aegerolysin-like proteins. It has been found that aegerolysin
#=GF CC   and ostreolysin are expressed during formation of primordia and
#=GF CC   fruiting bodies. It has been suggested that these haemolysins
#=GF CC   play an important role in initial phase of fungal fruiting. The
#=GF CC   bacterial members of this family are expressed during
#=GF CC   sporulation [1].  Ostreolysin was found cytolytic to various
#=GF CC   erythrocytes and tumour cells [2]. It forms transmembrane pores
#=GF CC   4 nm in diameter. The activity is inhibited by total membrane
#=GF CC   lipids, and modulated by lysophosphatides. The potential use of
#=GF CC   aegerolysins is reviewed [8] with special emphasis on their
#=GF CC   properties which would allow their use in therapeutics.
#=GF CC   Aegerolysin is part of the pleurotolysin pore-forming
#=GF CC   (Pleurotolysin) transporter superfamily. Member proteins
#=GF CC   assemble into a transmembrane pore complex [7].
